    </seriesStmt><sourceDesc><p>Born digital.</p></sourceDesc></fileDesc><revisionDesc><change who="http://syriaca.org/documentation/editors.xml#autogenerated" when="2026-07-28-05:00">CREATED: This bibl record was autogenerated from a Zotero record.</change><change who="https://www.zotero.org/cbss_admin" when="2022-11-18T14:31:36Z">Record created in Zotero.</change></revisionDesc></teiHeader><text><body><biblStruct type="journalArticle"><analytic><author><forename>J. Andrew</forename><surname>Doole</surname></author><title level="a">The Final Act of Paul: The Miracle of the Apostle’s Re-Attaching Head</title><idno type="URI">http://syriaca.org/cbss/4UK3DZMU</idno><idno type="URI">https://www.zotero.org/groups/a_comprehensive_bibliography_on_syriac_studies/items/4UK3DZMU</idno><idno type="URI">https://www.zotero.org/groups/4861694/items/4UK3DZMU</idno></analytic><monogr><title level="j">Apocrypha</title><imprint><date>2019</date></imprint><biblScope unit="vol">30</biblScope><biblScope unit="pp">87-106</biblScope></monogr><note type="abstract">There is a little-known tradition which attests to Paul’s decapitated head being lost just outside Rome and discovered much later by a shepherd. I will present four Syriac versions of this tradition, including the Letter of (Pseudo-)Dionysius the Areopagite to Timothy, a thirteenth century encyclopaedia by an East Syrian bishop called The Book of the Bee, and two hagiographic manuscripts. All of these attest to Paul’s head miraculously re-attaching to his body during a vigil/experiment in Rome. I will examine and compare these texts to see what motifs and possible historical events lie behind this unusual posthumous miracle.</note></biblStruct><bibl type="formatted" subtype="bibliography" resp="https://www.zotero.org/styles/chicago-note-bibliography-17th-edition">
